1
00:01:11,471 --> 00:01:15,587
A little further to the right, Tom.

2
00:01:15,587 --> 00:01:22,005
Is this OK?

3
00:01:22,005 --> 00:01:35,317
Good. What do you think, Richard?

4
00:01:35,317 --> 00:01:35,962
I like it there.

5
00:01:35,962 --> 00:02:00,091
It's the first thing people will see when they come in.

6
00:02:00,091 --> 00:02:07,604
It sets the tone for the whole show.

7
00:02:07,604 --> 00:02:10,360
The next thing they'll see is

8
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:14,675
this enlargement with the words Family Album, U.S.A. Family Album, U.S.A.

9
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:18,700
I can't believe this is really happening!

10
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:20,244
You've earned it.

11
00:02:20,244 --> 00:02:22,261
Years of work went into these pictures.

12
00:02:22,261 --> 00:02:24,692
I know, but it's still a dream come true.

13
00:02:24,692 --> 00:02:26,216
Well, remember,

14
00:02:26,216 --> 00:02:27,104
we're not sure

15
00:02:27,104 --> 00:02:30,378
what the critics are going to write about your show yet.

16
00:02:30,378 --> 00:02:31,562
And you never know

17
00:02:31,562 --> 00:02:34,156
what the man from the New York Times is going to say about it.

18
00:02:34,156 --> 00:02:34,854
Are you worried?

19
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:36,854
I always worry.

20
00:02:36,854 --> 00:02:37,832
The reviews of this show

21
00:02:37,832 --> 00:02:39,287
are important for the sales of your book.

22
00:02:39,287 --> 00:02:41,750
When do we see the reviews?

23
00:02:41,750 --> 00:02:42,063
Soon.

24
00:02:42,063 --> 00:02:44,899
One of the critics is coming over this morning for a preview.

25
00:02:44,899 --> 00:02:46,448
I hope he's in a good mood.

26
00:02:46,448 --> 00:02:47,766
So do I.

27
00:02:47,766 --> 00:02:53,449
Marilyn and I hope to use money from the sales of this book Marilyn

28
00:02:53,449 --> 00:02:54,358
to buy a new house.

29
00:02:54,358 --> 00:02:55,700
The book will be a success.

30
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:59,125
And the show will help promote it.

31
00:02:59,125 --> 00:03:01,656
Speaking of promoting the book,

32
00:03:01,656 --> 00:03:03,156
do I really have to

33
00:03:03,156 --> 00:03:05,834
autograph copies for the guests at the opening?

34
00:03:05,834 --> 00:03:07,612
It's common practice.

35
00:03:07,612 --> 00:03:09,612
I feel uncomfortable about it.

36
00:03:09,612 --> 00:03:11,562
A lot of people come to openings

37
00:03:11,562 --> 00:03:12,779
just so they can get

38
00:03:12,779 --> 00:03:15,307
the autograph of somebody who may be famous someday.

39
00:03:15,307 --> 00:03:17,185
Couldn't we wait until I'm famous?

40
00:03:17,185 --> 00:03:19,856
That might be sooner than you think.

41
00:03:19,856 --> 00:03:22,752
This one over here, Mr. Carlson?

42
00:03:22,752 --> 00:03:24,010
A little further back, Tom.

43
00:03:24,010 --> 00:03:26,261
It's too close to the refreshments.

44
00:03:26,261 --> 00:03:28,457
No, I think this one belongs in the "people-at-work" section.

45
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:31,435
You may be right.

46
00:03:31,435 --> 00:03:34,363
Try it there, Tom.

47
00:03:34,363 --> 00:03:34,563
Right.

48
00:03:34,413 --> 00:03:37,133
Nervous about the opening tonight?

49
00:03:37,133 --> 00:03:37,391
Nervous?

50
00:03:37,391 --> 00:03:42,089
No. I'm scared to death.

51
00:03:42,089 --> 00:03:43,347
How about guests?

52
00:03:43,347 --> 00:03:44,947
How many people will you be bringing?

53
00:03:44,947 --> 00:03:47,705
Well, my family, I guess.

54
00:03:47,705 --> 00:03:50,926
My wife Marilyn, my father and mother,

55
00:03:50,926 --> 00:03:52,612
my brother Robbie,

56
00:03:52,612 --> 00:03:55,259
my sister and her husband and their daughter.

57
00:03:55,259 --> 00:03:56,459
And my grandfather.

58
00:03:56,459 --> 00:03:57,539
Is that too many?

59
00:03:57,539 --> 00:04:01,215
No such thing as "too many" at an opening.

60
00:04:01,215 --> 00:04:03,215
I hope they like it.

61
00:04:03,215 --> 00:04:05,833
Your family or the public?

62
00:04:05,833 --> 00:04:07,473
Everybody.

63
00:04:07,473 --> 00:04:08,047
But especially my family.

64
00:04:08,047 --> 00:04:10,149
They've stood by me through all this.

65
00:04:10,149 --> 00:04:13,127
I'm sure they'll like it.

66
00:04:13,127 --> 00:04:15,527
Harvey, how are you? Harvey

67
00:04:15,527 --> 00:04:18,289
Mitchell, so nice of you to come. Mitchell

68
00:04:18,289 --> 00:04:20,428
Richard, this is Mitchell Johnson. Richard

69
00:04:20,428 --> 00:04:20,967
Mitchell is

70
00:04:20,967 --> 00:04:24,060
one of the most important syndicated reviewers in the country.

71
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:28,728
Well, you're the young man who did all this.

72
00:04:28,728 --> 00:04:28,928
I'm afraid so.

73
00:04:28,728 --> 00:04:31,294
Nice to meet you.

74
00:04:31,294 --> 00:04:34,601
Nice to meet you, Mr. Johnson.

75
00:04:34,601 --> 00:04:36,732
Mitchell has always encouraged new talent. Mitchell

76
00:04:36,732 --> 00:05:40,154
Well ... you mind if I look around

77
00:05:40,154 --> 00:05:44,715
and see what it says to me?

78
00:05:44,715 --> 00:05:45,731
Be our guest.

79
00:05:45,731 --> 00:05:47,171
Mm-hmm, hmm, uh-huh.

80
00:05:47,171 --> 00:05:51,241
What does that mean when he goes,

81
00:05:51,241 --> 00:05:53,399
"Mm-hmm, mm-hmm, mm-hmm"?

82
00:05:53,399 --> 00:05:55,477
It probably means he's clearing his throat.

83
00:05:55,477 --> 00:05:56,137
I don't know.

84
00:05:56,137 --> 00:05:58,784
I don't care what the critics say, Mr. Stewart.

85
00:05:58,784 --> 00:06:00,022
Your work is brilliant.

86
00:06:00,022 --> 00:06:00,525
Thank you, Tom.

87
00:06:00,525 --> 00:06:01,869
Tom is studying photography at NYU. Tom

88
00:06:01,869 --> 00:06:05,436
He's working with me during the summer months as an intern.

89
00:06:05,436 --> 00:06:05,686
Oh, really?

90
00:06:05,686 --> 00:06:06,509
I'd like to see your work.

91
00:06:06,509 --> 00:06:07,819
It's not good enough to show.

92
00:06:07,819 --> 00:06:09,267
I'm still learning.

93
00:06:09,267 --> 00:06:18,432
Oh, I'd still like to see your work.

94
00:06:18,432 --> 00:06:24,157
You may be the next Ansel Adams and not even know it.

95
00:06:24,157 --> 00:06:26,433
If you really mean it,

96
00:06:26,433 --> 00:06:29,884
I'll bring some of my pictures into the gallery.

97
00:06:29,884 --> 00:06:31,863
I do mean it.

98
00:06:31,863 --> 00:06:33,691
Very interesting pictures, Mr. Stewart.

99
00:06:33,691 --> 00:06:36,478
You have a most unusual eye.

100
00:06:36,478 --> 00:06:36,887
Thank you.

101
00:06:36,887 --> 00:06:37,909
I hope that's a compliment.

102
00:06:37,909 --> 00:06:38,373
It is.

103
00:06:38,373 --> 00:06:43,739
Are you going to be reviewing the show, Mitchell?

104
00:06:43,739 --> 00:06:45,583
Oh, yes. It's definitely worth reviewing.

105
00:06:45,583 --> 00:06:45,783
Favorably?

106
00:06:45,776 --> 00:07:07,348
Oh, you know I never answer that question, Harvey.

107
00:07:07,348 --> 00:07:12,661
I'd like an advance copy of the book, though,

108
00:07:12,661 --> 00:07:19,860
so I can study it.

109
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:23,716
I have an autographed one in my office.

110
00:07:23,716 --> 00:07:34,450
Tom, would you give Mr. Johnson Tom

111
00:07:34,450 --> 00:07:52,268
the copy of Richard's book on my desk?

112
00:07:52,268 --> 00:07:55,496
Just follow me, Mr. Johnson.

113
00:07:55,496 --> 00:07:56,474
Sure.

114
00:07:56,474 --> 00:08:01,452
Do you think he liked my photographs?

115
00:08:01,452 --> 00:08:05,407
We'll know when tonight's papers come out.

116
00:08:05,407 --> 00:08:08,407
Keep your fingers crossed.
